# 7. SORT 0, 1, 2

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

## PROBLEM

Sort:

```
[2,0,2,1,1,0]
```

Output:

```
[0,0,1,1,2,2]
```

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## FIRST OPTION

Counting:

```
count0
count1
count2
```

Then overwrite.

This is perfectly valid when values are exactly:

```
0,1,2
```

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## THREE-POINTER METHOD

Maintain:

```
low
mid
high
```

Regions:

```
[0 ... low-1]      → 0
[low ... mid-1]    → 1
[mid ... high]     → unknown
[high+1 ... n-1]   → 2
```

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## RULES

If:

```
a[mid] == 0
```

then:

```
swap(a[low], a[mid])
low++
mid++
```

If:

```
a[mid] == 1
```

then:

```
mid++
```

If:

```
a[mid] == 2
```

then:

```
swap(a[mid], a[high])
high--
```

IMPORTANT:

After swapping 2 with high:

```
DO NOT increment mid
```

because the new value at mid is unprocessed.

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## CODE

```
int low = 0;
int mid = 0;
int high = n - 1;

while(mid <= high) {

    if(a[mid] == 0) {

        swap(a[low], a[mid]);

        low++;
        mid++;
    }

    else if(a[mid] == 1) {

        mid++;
    }

    else {

        swap(a[mid], a[high]);

        high--;
    }
}
```

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## RECOGNITION

```
only 0,1,2
sort three values
colors / categories 0,1,2
```

Think:

```
DUTCH NATIONAL FLAG
```

