# 8. K LARGEST ELEMENTS — MIN HEAP

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

## WHY #8?

This is especially important because a July 7, 2026 reported TCS shift had:

```
Q1 → K Largest Elements using Min Heap
Q2 → Heap-based higher-level problem
```

So heap knowledge has appeared directly in the current cycle.

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## PROBLEM

Find K largest elements.

Input:

```
[3,2,1,5,6,4]
```

K:

```
2
```

Answer:

```
5,6
```

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## KEY IDEA

We only need:

```
K largest
```

We don't need to keep every element sorted.

Maintain a:

```
MIN HEAP of size K
```

Why min heap?

Because the smallest among our current K largest elements
must be removed when a bigger element arrives.

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## PROCESS

For each x:

```
push x
```

If heap size > K:

```
pop smallest
```

At the end:

```
heap contains K largest
```

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## CODE

```
priority_queue<
    int,
    vector<int>,
    greater<int>
> pq;

for(int x : a) {

    pq.push(x);

    if(pq.size() > k)
        pq.pop();
}
```

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## WHY MIN HEAP?

Suppose:

```
K = 3
```

Current:

```
10,20,30
```

Heap:

```
10
 ↓
smallest of selected K
```

New:

```
40
```

Remove:

```
10
```

Keep:

```
20,30,40
```

Therefore:

```
MIN HEAP
```

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## RECOGNITION

```
K largest
top K
K biggest
K highest values
```

Think:

```
MIN HEAP OF SIZE K
```

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## OPPOSITE

K smallest:

```
MAX HEAP of size K
```

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## COMPLEXITY

```
O(N log K)
```

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## SIMILAR

```
K smallest
Kth largest
Kth smallest
Top K frequent
Running K largest
```

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## MASTER CONNECTION

```
TOP K
  │
  ├── K LARGEST
  │      ↓
  │   MIN HEAP
  │
  └── K SMALLEST
         ↓
      MAX HEAP
```

