## FIRST THING TO UNDERSTAND

LeetCode-2289 style problems are about:

```
repeated removal
   +
elements becoming eligible
   +
multiple rounds
```

The naive approach:

```
repeatedly scan array
remove elements
repeat
```

can become:

```
O(N²)
```

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## CORE RECOGNITION

If question says:

```
remove elements based on neighbouring values
```

and:

```
removal happens in rounds
```

and:

```
N is large
```

ask:

```
"Can I avoid simulating every round?"
```

Often:

```
MONOTONIC STACK
or
DP + STACK
```

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## IMPORTANT

Do NOT memorize one LeetCode-2289 implementation blindly.

TCS may alter:

```
neighbour condition
direction
removal rule
output
```

The reusable skill is:

```
MONOTONIC RELATION
   +
STACK
   +
STATE / DAYS
```

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## BASIC MONOTONIC STACK THINKING

For a decreasing/increasing relation:

```
while stack condition violated:

    pop
```

Then:

```
push current
```

The stack represents:

```
elements still relevant
for future comparisons
```

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## GENERIC TEMPLATE

```
stack< pair<int,int> > st;

for(int x : a) {

    int state = ...;

    while(!st.empty() &&
          condition(st.top(), x)) {

        // resolve old element

        st.pop();
    }

    st.push({x, state});
}
```

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## MASTER QUESTION

Ask:

```
"Which previous elements are no longer useful?"
```

If an element can never become useful again:

```
POP IT.
```

That is the heart of monotonic stacks.

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

